Two notes on accuracy:

Service-entry year depends on scope. The base Bushmaster entered Australian service in the mid-2000s; the New Zealand variant in this image entered service in 2023. 
The challenge is keyed to the vehicle in the photo, so 2023 is the intended answer — but state which you're giving.
Confirm the capacity figure. I have not verified the exact number HTB accepts. Read it from the manufacturer's spec sheet and match the challenge's expected format rather than submitting a remembered value.

Submit the five answers on the Challenge page; 100% marks the challenge Owned.

Root cause (scenario lesson):

This is public-information disclosure through published imagery, not a technical exploit. A single unredacted operational photo, shared on an official social-media account, is enough to identify a specific platform and pull its full specification sheet from open sources.
There is no "attack chain" — the data was already public; OSINT just assembled it.

Defensive takeaways:
Review imagery before publishing. Treat operational photos as potential intelligence: strip EXIF/GPS metadata, and check backgrounds for installations, signage, unit markings, and identifiable equipment.
Mind the mosaic effect. Individually harmless posts (a vehicle here, a location tag there) combine into a precise picture. Train staff on aggregation risk.
Run periodic self-OSINT. Reverse-image-search your own public media to see what an outside analyst could reconstruct.

Note: "detecting" that an outsider reverse-image-searched a public photo isn't feasible — the image is already public. The control is at publication time, not detection.

Key lessons:
Catalog visual features before searching; they drive both the reverse-image query and the sanity check on matches.
Use more than one reverse-image engine — TinEye, Google, and Yandex surface different sources.
Corroborate specifications from the manufacturer or an encyclopedic source, not from the photo or an AI summary alone.

